Also written ''Eolian'' and ''Aiolian.''

¶'''Æolic''' (ē-ol′ik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''Æolicus'', < Gr. Αἰολικός, of or pertaining to Æolis or the Æolians: see ''Æolian''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Pertaining to Æolis or Æolia, to the Æolians, or to Æolus, their mythical ancestor; Æolian: as, ''Æolic'' towns; the ''Æolic'' branch of the Greek race.

{{smaller block|That Dicaiarchus was correct is proved by an examination of the peculiar position occupied by the traces of ''Aiolic'' influence in Homer. {{right|''Amer. Jour. Philol.'', VII. 232.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Æolic dialect''', one of the three great dialects or groups of subdialects of ancient Greek, the others being the Doric and Ionic. It was spoken in Æolis and many other Greek countries, and is important as the dialect used by the Lesbian poets Sappho, Alcæus, etc.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' The language of the Æolians; the Æolian dialect of Greek.

Also written ''Eolic'' and ''Aiolic.''

'''æolid, æolidid''' (ē′ō-lid, ē-ol′i-did), ''n.'' A member of the ''Æolidæ'' or ''Æolididæ.''

'''Æolidæ''' (ē-ol′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' Same as ''Æolididæ.''

